<main class="container">

  <section class="section" id="what">
    <div class="section-tag">Understanding Hemodialysis</div>
    <h2>What Dialysis Does — and What It Cannot</h2>
    <div class="intro-callout">
    <div class="illus-wrap" style="background:#f9fafb;border:1px solid #e2e6eb;border-radius:14px;padding:24px;margin:24px 0;text-align:center;">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 540 170" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" style="max-width:520px;width:100%;">
  <text x="270" y="16" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#0f1e2e">What Happens During a Hemodialysis Session</text>
  <!-- Steps as connected boxes -->
  <rect x="10" y="36" width="90" height="90" rx="8" fill="#e1f5f0" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="55" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22">🩸</text>
  <text x="55" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="#1a6b72">Access</text>
  <text x="55" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">Fistula / graft /</text>
  <text x="55" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">catheter</text>
  <text x="55" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">connected</text>

  <line x1="100" y1="81" x2="118" y2="81" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
  <defs><marker id="ar" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" refX="5" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L0,6 L7,3 z" fill="#1a6b72"/></marker></defs>

  <rect x="121" y="36" width="90" height="90" rx="8" fill="#e1f5f0" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="166" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22">⚙️</text>
  <text x="166" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="#1a6b72">Machine</text>
  <text x="166" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">Pumps blood at</text>
  <text x="166" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">300 mL/min</text>
  <text x="166" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">through filter</text>

  <line x1="211" y1="81" x2="229" y2="81" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>

  <rect x="232" y="36" width="90" height="90" rx="8" fill="#e1f5f0" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="277" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22">🔬</text>
  <text x="277" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="#1a6b72">Dialyzer</text>
  <text x="277" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">Hollow fibers</text>
  <text x="277" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">filter urea, K+,</text>
  <text x="277" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">phosphorus</text>

  <line x1="322" y1="81" x2="340" y2="81" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>

  <rect x="343" y="36" width="90" height="90" rx="8" fill="#e1f5f0" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5"/>
  <text x="388" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22">💧</text>
  <text x="388" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="#1a6b72">Fluid removal</text>
  <text x="388" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">Ultrafiltration</text>
  <text x="388" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">removes excess</text>
  <text x="388" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" fill="#1e2a38">fluid (UF goal)</text>

  <line x1="433" y1="81" x2="451" y2="81" stroke="#1a6b72" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>

  <rect x="454" y="36" width="76" height="90" rx="8" fill="#1a6b72"/>
  <text x="492" y="60" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" fill="white">🩸</text>
  <text x="492" y="78" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="white">Cleaned</text>
  <text x="492" y="91" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.8)">blood returns</text>
  <text x="492" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.8)">to body</text>
  <text x="492" y="117" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="rgba(255,255,255,.8)">4 hrs · 3×/wk</text>

  <!-- Targets bar -->
  <rect x="10" y="136" width="520" height="24" rx="7" fill="#f9fafb" stroke="#e2e6eb" stroke-width="1"/>
  <text x="270" y="151" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#1e2a38">KDIGO targets: spKt/V ≥1.4 · Hgb 100–115 g/L · BP &lt;140/90 pre-HD · K+ 3.5–5.5 · Phos 3.5–5.5 mg/dL</text>
</svg>
          </div>
      <p class="illus-caption">Each hemodialysis session removes approximately 2 kg of fluid and clears toxins your kidneys can no longer excrete. Missing sessions allows dangerous build-up of potassium and fluid between treatments.</p>

      <p>Dialysis is a <strong>life-sustaining treatment</strong> that replaces the filtering function of your failed kidneys. Three times a week, a machine removes waste products, excess fluid, and dangerous electrolytes from your blood — preventing the toxin buildup that would otherwise be fatal. It is not a cure, but it allows you to <strong>live actively, work, and be with your family.</strong></p>
    </div>
    <p>Hemodialysis (HD) uses a dialyzer — often called an artificial kidney — to filter your blood outside your body. Blood passes through millions of tiny hollow fibers surrounded by dialysis fluid (dialysate). Waste and fluid move across the membrane; clean blood returns to you.</p>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>What dialysis does well</h4>
        <p>Removes uremic toxins (urea, creatinine), controls potassium and acid levels, removes excess fluid, and prevents life-threatening complications of kidney failure. When adequate (spKt/V ≥1.4), it significantly reduces hospitalizations and mortality.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-amber"></span>What dialysis cannot replace</h4>
        <p>Dialysis does not produce erythropoietin (causing anemia), activate vitamin D (causing bone disease), or remove all uremic toxins (especially protein-bound ones). These require additional medications and supplements alongside dialysis.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="num-grid">
      <div class="num-card">
        <div class="n-val">3x</div>
        <div class="n-label">Sessions per week (MWF or TThS)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num-card">
        <div class="n-val">4 hrs</div>
        <div class="n-label">Minimum session duration for adequate clearance</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num-card">
        <div class="n-val">≥1.4</div>
        <div class="n-label">spKt/V — your dialysis adequacy target (KDIGO 2024)</div>
        <div class="n-target">Measured monthly</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num-card">
        <div class="n-val">&lt;2.5 kg</div>
        <div class="n-label">Ideal interdialytic weight gain between sessions</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="session">
    <div class="section-tag">Your HD Session</div>
    <h2>What Happens During Each Session</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Arrival and assessment</h4>
          <p>Your weight, blood pressure, heart rate, and temperature are checked. Your weight is compared to your dry weight — the difference tells the team how much fluid to remove. Always tell the nurse about any symptoms you had since your last session.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Access connection</h4>
          <p>Two needles are inserted into your AV fistula (or your catheter is connected). One draws blood out; one returns it. This is the most uncomfortable part for most patients — tell your nurse if you are anxious or in pain.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Filtration — 4 hours</h4>
          <p>Blood circulates through the dialyzer continuously. You can read, watch TV, sleep, or talk. Avoid sleeping through the entire session as staff need to monitor you. Blood pressure may drop (intradialytic hypotension) — report dizziness or cramping immediately.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Disconnection and post-HD check</h4>
          <p>Needles are removed and pressure applied to the fistula sites. Post-HD weight and BP are checked. Do not stand up quickly — blood pressure is often lower after HD. Sit for a few minutes first.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-amber">
      <div class="alert-icon">⚠</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Never miss or shorten a session</h4>
        <p>Each missed HD session allows toxins and potassium to accumulate dangerously. Missing even one session significantly increases the risk of life-threatening hyperkalemia, fluid overload, and cardiac arrest. If you cannot come for any reason, call your dialysis center immediately.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="access">
    <div class="section-tag">Vascular Access</div>
    <h2>Protecting Your AV Fistula — Your Lifeline</h2>
    <p>Your AV fistula (arteriovenous fistula) is created surgically by connecting an artery to a vein, usually in your forearm. This creates a high-flow vessel that can withstand repeated needle insertions. It is your most important asset as a dialysis patient.</p>

    <div class="alert alert-teal">
      <div class="alert-icon">💡</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>How to check your fistula every day</h4>
        <p>Place two fingers gently over your fistula and feel for a "thrill" — a vibrating buzz. You can also listen with your ear close to the skin for a "bruit" — a whooshing sound. If the thrill or bruit is absent or weaker than usual, go to your dialysis center or ER immediately. This may indicate clotting.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-green"></span>DO — protect your fistula</h4>
        <p>Check the thrill daily. Keep the skin clean and moisturized. Report any redness, swelling, or warmth early. Sleep on the opposite arm. Exercise the fistula arm with a squeeze ball to keep it strong.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>DO NOT — never do these</h4>
        <p>Never allow blood pressure measurement on the fistula arm. Never allow IV lines or blood draws on that arm. Never wear tight clothing, watches, or jewelry on that arm. Never sleep with your full weight on it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert alert-red">
      <div class="alert-icon">🚨</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Signs of fistula problems — go immediately</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>No thrill or bruit felt or heard</li>
          <li>Arm is swollen, red, hot, or painful</li>
          <li>Bleeding that does not stop within 20 minutes of firm pressure</li>
          <li>Fever with the fistula arm swollen (possible infection)</li>
          <li>Coldness or numbness of the hand on the fistula side</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="targets">
    <div class="section-tag">KDIGO 2024 Targets</div>
    <h2>Your Key Laboratory Goals</h2>
    <table class="lab-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Target</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hemoglobin</td><td><span class="target-badge">100–115 g/L</span></td><td>Anemia causes fatigue, heart strain, poor quality of life. Requires ESA + iron</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ferritin / TSAT</td><td><span class="target-badge">Ferritin &gt;200 / TSAT &gt;30%</span></td><td>Iron must be adequate for ESA to work. IV iron (Iron Sucrose) preferred in HD</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Potassium (pre-HD)</td><td><span class="target-badge">3.5–5.5 mEq/L</span></td><td>High K causes dangerous cardiac arrhythmias. Diet and medication control essential</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phosphorus</td><td><span class="target-badge">3.5–5.5 mg/dL</span></td><td>High phosphorus causes itching, bone disease, and vessel calcification</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Calcium</td><td><span class="target-badge">8.4–10.2 mg/dL</span></td><td>Low calcium causes muscle cramps and bone loss; high calcium increases calcification</td></tr>
        <tr><td>iPTH</td><td><span class="target-badge">130–600 pg/mL</span></td><td>Parathyroid overactivity (secondary hyperparathyroidism) damages bones and heart</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Albumin</td><td><span class="target-badge">≥ 4.0 g/dL</span></td><td>Low albumin = protein malnutrition = poor survival. Protein intake must be adequate</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bicarbonate</td><td><span class="target-badge">22–26 mEq/L</span></td><td>Metabolic acidosis accelerates muscle wasting and bone disease</td></tr>
        <tr><td>spKt/V</td><td><span class="target-badge">≥ 1.4</span></td><td>Your dialysis adequacy score — must not be allowed to fall below this</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blood pressure (pre-HD)</td><td><span class="target-badge">&lt; 140/90 mmHg</span></td><td>Hypertension is the leading cause of cardiovascular death in dialysis patients</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="diet">
    <div class="section-tag">Renal Diet for HD Patients</div>
    <h2>What to Eat — and What to Avoid</h2>
    <p>Diet on hemodialysis is different from pre-dialysis CKD. Because dialysis removes some nutrients, your protein needs are <em>higher</em> — but potassium, phosphorus, and sodium must still be controlled carefully.</p>

    <div class="hd-day-grid">
      <div class="day-card hd-day">
        <h4>HD Day — slightly more liberal</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Dialysis will remove excess potassium today</li>
          <li>You may eat a small pre-HD meal (light, low-potassium)</li>
          <li>Take phosphate binders WITH your meal — not before or after</li>
          <li>IV iron and ESA given during session</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="day-card non-hd-day">
        <h4>Non-HD Day — strict control</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Potassium and fluid accumulate — be most careful today</li>
          <li>Stick strictly to your fluid limit</li>
          <li>No high-potassium foods (coconut water, banana, kamote)</li>
          <li>Continue all oral medications as prescribed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table class="lab-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Nutrient</th><th>Target</th><th>Avoid / Limit</th><th>Safe choices</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Protein</td>
          <td>1.0–1.4 g/kg/day</td>
          <td>Processed meats, longganisa, tocino (high sodium + phosphorus)</td>
          <td>Egg whites, fish (bangus, tilapia), chicken breast, tofu</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Potassium</td>
          <td>&lt; 2,000 mg/day</td>
          <td>Coconut water, banana, kamote, squash, tomato, avocado, dried fruits</td>
          <td>White rice, cabbage, green beans, apple, pineapple, grapes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Phosphorus</td>
          <td>800–1,000 mg/day</td>
          <td>Dark colas, processed cheese, nuts, organ meats, canned fish with bones</td>
          <td>Fresh (not canned) fish, egg whites, rice, bread, corn</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sodium</td>
          <td>&lt; 2,000 mg/day</td>
          <td>Patis, toyo, bagoong, instant noodles, chips, canned goods</td>
          <td>Fresh cooked food, herbs, garlic, calamansi for flavor</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="alert alert-green">
      <div class="alert-icon">🥗</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Leaching vegetables to reduce potassium</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Peel and cut vegetables into small pieces</li>
          <li>Soak in a large amount of water for at least 2 hours (change water once)</li>
          <li>Drain, then boil in fresh water — discard the boiling water</li>
          <li>This reduces potassium content by 30–50% in most vegetables</li>
          <li>Works for: kamote, potato, pechay, kangkong, sitaw</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="fluids">
    <div class="section-tag">Fluid Management</div>
    <h2>Controlling Your Fluid Intake</h2>
    <p>Fluid builds up between dialysis sessions because your kidneys can no longer produce urine. Too much fluid causes swollen legs, shortness of breath, high blood pressure, and dangerous strain on your heart. Your goal is to gain <strong>less than 2.5 kg between sessions</strong> (ideally less than 1 kg per day).</p>

    <div class="alert alert-teal">
      <div class="alert-icon">💧</div>
      <div class="alert-body">
        <h4>Your fluid allowance = urine output + 500 mL</h4>
        <p>If you make very little or no urine, your daily fluid limit is approximately 500–800 mL (about 2–3 cups). This includes ALL fluids: water, soup, juice, coffee, tea, ice cream, gelatin, and even the water in fruits and vegetables. Ask your nurse to help you calculate your personal limit.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="three-col">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-teal"></span>Control thirst</h4>
        <p>Thirst is driven by sodium. The less salt you eat, the less thirsty you will be. Suck on ice chips (count as fluid), chew sugarless gum, or rinse your mouth with cold water and spit it out.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-navy"></span>Weigh daily</h4>
        <p>Weigh yourself every morning after using the toilet and before eating. Use the same scale. Record it. If you gain more than 1 kg overnight, reduce fluid immediately and inform your dialysis team.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-card">
        <h4><span class="dot dot-red"></span>Hidden fluids</h4>
        <p>Many patients forget: soup broth, sabaw, ice, halo-halo, softdrinks, beer, watermelon, and cucumber are all significant fluid sources. Track everything that is liquid or melts at room temperature.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="daily-life">
    <div class="section-tag">Living Well on Dialysis</div>
    <h2>Daily Life, Work, and Emotional Health</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Exercise is safe and beneficial</h4>
          <p>Regular light exercise — walking, stretching, gentle cycling — improves dialysis adequacy, reduces fatigue, and lifts mood. Even 20–30 minutes of walking on non-HD days significantly improves cardiovascular outcomes. Avoid heavy lifting on the fistula arm.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Many patients work and travel</h4>
          <p>Dialysis is available in all major cities across the Philippines and internationally. If you plan to travel, arrange HD sessions at your destination 4–6 weeks in advance. Your nephrologist can provide a medical summary letter for guest dialysis arrangements.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Vaccinations are essential</h4>
          <p>Dialysis patients are immunocompromised. Required vaccines: Hepatitis B (double-dose 40 mcg at 0, 1, 6 months — check anti-HBs titer annually), Influenza (annually), Pneumococcal (PCV13 then PPSV23). Check your vaccination status at your next visit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h4>Mental health matters</h4>
          <p>Depression affects up to 40% of dialysis patients. Feelings of loss, grief, fatigue, and dependency are normal and valid. Talk to your nephrologist — referral to a counselor or psychiatrist is appropriate and available. Your family's support is your most powerful medicine.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="red-flags">
    <div class="section-tag">Emergency Warning Signs</div>
    <h2>When to Seek Immediate Care</h2>
    <div class="red-flags">
      <h3>⚠ Go to the ER or call your dialysis center immediately if:</h3>
      <div class="red-flags-grid">
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>No thrill or bruit in your fistula</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Fistula bleeding that won't stop in 20 minutes</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Severe shortness of breath or cannot lie flat</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Chest pain or irregular heartbeat</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Muscle weakness or paralysis (high potassium)</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Fever with chills and shaking (catheter infection)</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Confusion or inability to wake up</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Weight gain of more than 3 kg since last session</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Seizures</div>
        <div class="red-flag-item"><div class="rf-dot"></div>Missed session with any of the above symptoms</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="disclaimer"><strong><span data-lang="en">Important</span><span data-lang="tl" class="lang-hidden">Mahalagang Paalala</span><span data-lang="ceb" class="lang-hidden">Importante nga Pahibalo</span>:</strong> This guide is for educational purposes and does not replace individualized medical advice from your nephrologist and dialysis care team.</div>

    <div class="dr-card">
      <div class="dr-avatar" style="width:64px;height:64px;border-radius:50%;overflow:hidden;flex-shrink:0;border:2px solid rgba(184,150,46,0.5);background:#0f1e2e;">
    <img src="../images/photo-contact.jpg" alt="Dr. W. G. M. Rivero" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;object-position:center top;">
  </div>
